<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palatinate-Neuburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1505">1505</a>–<a href="/years/1808">1808</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Pfalz-Neuburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palatinate-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a> was a Wittelsbach principality of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, created in 1505 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> War of Succession and centred on Neuburg an der Donau. Its ruler Wolfgang William became a Catholic in 1613 and gained Jülich and Berg in 1614, and in 1685 Philip William of Neuburg inherited the Electoral Palatinate. The Neuburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> ruled until 1742, when the line died out and Charles Theodore of Sulzbach succeeded.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg.jpg" alt="Palatinate-Neuburg" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_Palatinate-Neuburg_(3%5E2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Palatinate-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a> was a territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> founded in 1505 by a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a>, with its capital at Neuburg an der Donau. It covered about 2,750 square kilometres and had a population of some 100,000. After the Verdict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> ended the Landshut War of Succession, the duchy was created from the territories north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> for Otto Henry and Philipp, the sons of Ruprecht of the Palatinate. While they were minors their grandfather Philip, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, ruled it until his death in 1508, followed by Elector Frederick II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1541 Count Palatine Otto Henry converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>, and his palace chapel at Neuburg Castle, consecrated on 25 April 1543 by the theologian Andreas Osiander, was the first newly built Protestant church. In 1556 he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> and the next year ceded the duchy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfgang-count-palatine-of-zweibrucken">Wolfgang, Count Palatine of Zweibrücken</a>. Wolfgang's eldest son Philipp Louis founded the elder line of Palatine Zweibrücken-Neuburg in 1569, and the Sulzbach line separated from it in 1614.</p><p>Palatinate-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-union">Protestant Union</a> in 1608. When the Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg fell into dispute in 1609 after the death of the childless John William, Count Palatine Wolfgang Wilhelm claimed them and fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-julich-succession">War of the Jülich Succession</a>, converting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> midway to secure alliances. Neuburg and the Duchies of Jülich and Berg were then held in personal union, formalized in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-xanten">Treaty of Xanten</a>. Philip William became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> in 1685, as did his successors Johann Wilhelm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-philip-elector-palatine">Charles Philip</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>When Elector Charles Philip died in 1742, all his territories, Palatinate-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a> among them, passed to the Palatinate-Sulzbach line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a>, in the person of Charles Theodore, a descendant of Augustus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> of Sulzbach, a brother of Wolfgang Wilhelm. In 1800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invaded the duchy, and on 26 June a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, Württemberg and Bavarian force fought a battle there and withdrew after most of a day. General Ney then made his headquarters in the castle at Neuburg. The duchy was abolished in 1808. After the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1837 it was joined with Swabia, and it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a> in the 1970s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palatinate-Neuburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Wittelsbachs repeated partitions weakened the duchy for two and a half centuries, until the Landshut War of Succession ended with the Cologne arbitration of 1505, which reunited most of the lands under Albert IV, though the <b>Palatinate-Neuburg</b> was carved out for his rivals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Landshut War of Succession (1503–1505), fought over the claims of Elector Philip's son Rupert to the Bavarian inheritance, ended in defeat, the loss of lands to Bavaria and Württemberg and the creation of the smaller <b>Palatinate-Neuburg</b> for Rupert's sons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Xanten in 1614 gave Jülich and Berg to <b>Palatinate-Neuburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-julich">Duchy of Jülich</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Xanten in 1614 divided the lands: Cleves, Mark and Ravensberg went to Brandenburg, and Jülich and Berg to <b>Palatinate-Neuburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-duchies-of-julich-cleves-berg">United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg</a></li></ul></section>
